course: dictionary
lesson: 9131
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word snag can mean a small problem, a catch on cloth, or even a sausage. Read the words around it first: «The only snag is that it's raining.» The rest of the sentence is about rain spoiling a plan, so a snag here is a problem.
explain: «Let's go to the servo for a snag!» In Dublin, Rian's cousins from Australia say this, and Rian is confused. A servo is a petrol station, and people go there to eat, so a snag must be a sausage. Ask yourself: which meaning would fit in this sentence? A sausage fits; a problem does not.
explain: Snag can also be a verb, a doing word. To snag something means to catch it on something sharp, or to grab it quickly. Put each meaning into the sentence and see which one still makes sense.
fact: Snag can be a noun (a snag) or a verb (to snag).
fact: A snag is a small hidden problem or difficulty, as in «hit a snag».
fact: A snag can also be a sharp or rough bit that catches cloth, leaving a pull or tear in a jumper.
fact: In Australia, a snag is a sausage, often cooked on a barbecue and sold in bread at a sausage sizzle.
fact: Informal: to snag something means to get it quickly, like snagging the last seat.
exercise: In «The only snag is that it's raining», what does snag mean? | *A small hidden problem | A sharp bit that tears cloth | A quick win | A sausage cooked on a barbecue
exercise: Rian's cousins say, «Let's go to the servo for a snag!» What is a snag here? | A tear in a jumper | *A sausage, often cooked on a barbecue | A petrol station | A hidden problem
exercise: «I snagged my jumper on the old fence.» What does snagged mean? | Found quickly and easily | Knitted with a new pattern | *Caught and torn on something sharp | Washed and dried
test: «She snagged the last ticket before anyone else.» What does snagged mean here? | Tore on a sharp edge | Dropped on the floor | Lost in the post | *Got quickly, before anyone else
test: Sam's camping plan «hit a snag» when the tent went missing. What does hit a snag mean? | Was caught on a nail | *Met a problem | Got a prize | Ate a sausage at the campsite
exam: Snag has several meanings. What is the best way to find the right one? | Skip the word and never come back to it | Guess the first meaning you know | Ask the nearest person without reading the sentence | *Read the words around it and check which meaning fits
